1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,330
No calculator.

2
00:00:01,330 --> 00:00:07,410
Find the cube root of one hundred and twenty seven, correct to four decimal places.

3
00:00:07,410 --> 00:00:09,032
It sounds impossible by hand.

4
00:00:09,233 --> 00:00:14,951
By the end of this lesson you will do it in three lines: five point zero two six five.

5
00:00:14,951 --> 00:00:21,508
And the tool that does it will also expand a to the power twenty in one go, and give you the number e.

6
00:00:27,333 --> 00:00:32,064
Welcome to Class 12 Mathematics, Unit 2: the Binomial Theorem.

7
00:00:34,433 --> 00:00:37,794
The theorem for whole number powers, and its proof.

8
00:00:37,794 --> 00:00:41,778
The general term, the middle term and the coefficients.

9
00:00:41,778 --> 00:00:45,776
The theorem for any index, as an infinite series.

10
00:00:45,776 --> 00:00:47,234
Approximations.

11
00:00:47,234 --> 00:00:48,614
Euler's number e.

12
00:00:48,614 --> 00:00:54,452
And the series for e to the x, a to the x, and log of one plus x.

13
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:03,085
Multiply out the first few powers of a plus b, and watch.

14
00:01:03,085 --> 00:01:05,755
The power of a falls from n to zero.

15
00:01:05,755 --> 00:01:08,776
The power of b rises from zero to n.

16
00:01:08,776 --> 00:01:10,390
They always add to n.

17
00:01:10,390 --> 00:01:18,880
And the coefficients, one two one, one three three one, one four six four one, are the rows of Pascal's triangle.

18
00:01:20,100 --> 00:01:33,448
So the binomial theorem says: a plus b to the n is the sum, from r equals zero to n, of n C r times a to the n minus r times b to the r. Exactly n plus one terms.

19
00:01:34,667 --> 00:01:36,088
Why combinations?

20
00:01:36,088 --> 00:01:41,622
Because a plus b to the fifth is five brackets multiplied together.

21
00:01:41,622 --> 00:01:47,390
To make a term a cubed b squared, you take b from two of the brackets and a from the other three.

22
00:01:47,390 --> 00:01:52,468
The number of ways to choose those two brackets is five C two, ten.

23
00:01:52,468 --> 00:01:55,194
So a cubed b squared appears ten times.

24
00:01:57,400 --> 00:01:59,836
The formal proof is by induction.

25
00:01:59,836 --> 00:02:03,307
For n equal to one it reads a plus b. True.

26
00:02:03,533 --> 00:02:08,890
Now assume it is true for some k, and multiply both sides by a plus b.

27
00:02:09,100 --> 00:02:13,646
Shift the index in the second sum so both sums have the same shape.

28
00:02:13,646 --> 00:02:17,840
The middle terms pair up as k C r plus k C r minus one.

29
00:02:18,067 --> 00:02:22,925
By Pascal's rule from unit one, that is k plus one C r.

30
00:02:22,925 --> 00:02:29,270
So the formula holds for k plus one, and by induction for every positive integer n.

31
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:33,327
Keep the minus sign inside the bracket.

32
00:02:33,327 --> 00:02:36,517
For a minus b, the signs alternate.

33
00:02:36,517 --> 00:02:45,211
And for two x minus three y, the whole of minus three y is the second quantity, so raise the three to the power as well.

34
00:02:46,433 --> 00:02:47,906
A first expansion.

35
00:02:47,906 --> 00:02:54,984
a is two x, b is three over x, and the coefficients are one five ten ten five one.

36
00:02:55,200 --> 00:02:57,599
Write every term with its powers.

37
00:02:57,800 --> 00:02:59,338
Then simplify each one.

38
00:03:01,367 --> 00:03:02,183
A neat trick.

39
00:03:02,600 --> 00:03:08,897
Subtract the two expansions: terms with even powers of b cancel, odd ones double.

40
00:03:09,100 --> 00:03:16,016
With a squared three and a to the four nine: two times seventy six, one hundred and fifty two.

41
00:03:16,016 --> 00:03:17,002
The surds vanish.

42
00:03:22,600 --> 00:03:25,232
Any single term can be written directly.

43
00:03:25,232 --> 00:03:42,556
The general term, T r plus one, is n C r, a to the n minus r, b to the r. Notice the plus one: r equal to zero gives the first term, so the term carrying r is the r plus one th. Write the plus one down every time, or you will be one term out.

44
00:03:42,767 --> 00:03:45,125
There are n plus one terms.

45
00:03:45,125 --> 00:03:51,831
If n is even that is an odd number, and there is one middle term, number n over two plus one.

46
00:03:51,831 --> 00:03:54,049
If n is odd, there are two middle terms.

47
00:03:55,267 --> 00:03:58,315
The most common question: the term independent of x.

48
00:03:58,533 --> 00:04:05,301
Write the general term, and collect all the powers of x into one: x to the eighteen minus three r.

49
00:04:05,533 --> 00:04:11,160
Independent of x means that power is zero: r is six, the seventh term.

50
00:04:11,367 --> 00:04:17,665
Its value: eighty four times sixty four, five thousand three hundred and seventy six.

51
00:04:19,667 --> 00:04:20,449
A coefficient.

52
00:04:20,900 --> 00:04:24,514
General term: x to the sixty minus seven r.

53
00:04:24,733 --> 00:04:30,651
Set that equal to thirty two: r is four, a whole number, so the term exists.

54
00:04:30,867 --> 00:04:36,039
The coefficient is fifteen C four: one thousand three hundred and sixty five.

55
00:04:38,067 --> 00:04:38,808
Middle terms.

56
00:04:39,300 --> 00:04:42,804
Eight is even: one middle term, the fifth.

57
00:04:42,804 --> 00:04:45,364
The x's cancel completely: seventy.

58
00:04:45,567 --> 00:04:49,462
Seven is odd: two middle terms, the fourth and fifth.

59
00:04:49,462 --> 00:04:53,068
The fourth is minus five sixty x to the four y cubed.

60
00:04:53,300 --> 00:04:56,707
The fifth is two eighty x cubed y to the four.

61
00:04:58,733 --> 00:05:10,271
Put x equal to one in one plus x to the n: the coefficients add up to two to the n. Put x equal to minus one: the alternating sum is zero.

62
00:05:10,271 --> 00:05:17,446
And in general, the sum of the coefficients of any expansion is found by putting every variable equal to one.

63
00:05:17,446 --> 00:05:22,738
For two x minus three y to the twelve: minus one to the twelve, which is one.

64
00:05:23,967 --> 00:05:25,158
The ratio method again.

65
00:05:25,367 --> 00:05:32,471
The ratio of consecutive coefficients is n minus r plus one over r. Equal to eight thirds.

66
00:05:32,700 --> 00:05:35,388
The next ratio, equal to seven quarters.

67
00:05:35,600 --> 00:05:38,870
Subtract: n is ten and r is three.

68
00:05:38,870 --> 00:05:41,353
The terms are the third, fourth and fifth.

69
00:05:43,967 --> 00:05:45,349
Exam tip.

70
00:05:45,349 --> 00:05:56,078
For any particular term, write T r plus one, collect every power of x into a single exponent, and set it equal to the power you want.

71
00:05:56,078 --> 00:05:59,688
If r does not come out a whole number, the term simply does not exist.

